# os_connect(host_string, port) -> SOCKET on success, -1 on failure.
# Resolves host via getaddrinfo (AF_UNSPEC/SOCK_STREAM/IPPROTO_TCP
# hints, arbitrary hostname or numeric IP -- not a numeric-IP-only
# shortcut), patches the resolved sockaddr's port field directly
# (sin_port/sin6_port share the same offset+2 in both sockaddr_in and
# sockaddr_in6, so one patch covers both families) since getaddrinfo's
# own pServiceName mechanism would need a decimal-string port this
# backend has no ready int-to-string routine for, then creates the
# socket using the family/socktype/protocol getaddrinfo itself
# resolved (so it's never wrong for the family actually returned).
make a function called x64_os_connect_asm returns asm
  let suffix = label_asm_safe(0)
  let sb = sb_new()
  # Real bug found and fixed here: this used to pop THREE stack values
  # (host_addr, host_len, port), as if the caller pre-split the host
  # string into a raw address/length pair -- but the actual CallHost
  # site (os_connect(host_string, port), called from tcp_connect) only
  # ever pushes TWO values, the tagged host STRING and the port. Popping
  # an extra value read one slot too far down the real value stack,
  # corrupting whatever the caller's own earlier expression had there
  # (confirmed directly: segfaulted on the very next real call). Fixed
  # to unmask the tagged string itself, same [len][bytes] convention
  # os_send's own r13/r14 unmasking already uses.
